# Environment for the SEPARATE observability stack (observability/docker-compose.yml) - the wiring
# used when the sidecar runs on the host under `dotnet run`. Copy it next to that compose file:
#
#   cp observability/.env.example observability/.env
#   docker compose -f observability/docker-compose.yml up -d
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS HERE AND NOT AT THE REPO ROOT. Compose resolves the default `.env` from the
# project directory, and for `-f observability/docker-compose.yml` that is THIS directory. A
# repo-root `.env` is simply not read by that command, so the credential below appears to be set and
# Grafana still comes up on admin/admin without saying so - the failure this file exists to prevent.
# Keep the credential here and the documented bring-up needs no flag; keep it in the repo-root `.env`
# instead and every bring-up has to pass `--env-file .env`, which also means the file must exist -
# `--env-file` is required, unlike the default `.env`, and a clean clone has neither.
#
# The repo-root `.env.example` stays the home for the OVERLAY wiring
# (docker-compose.observability.yml), whose first compose file is at the repo root; it reads a
# repo-root `.env` with no flag for the same reason this one does not.
#
# reference: DEPLOYMENT.md § "Observability: two wirings"

# Grafana's admin login, passed through to GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER / GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD.
# Prometheus, Loki and Tempo have no auth of their own, and Loki holds application logs and Tempo
# spans, so this login and the 127.0.0.1 binding are the whole of the access control. The values below are the compose
# fallbacks, not a recommendation: they are safe for a local run and nowhere else. CHANGE THEM in
# the same edit as any change that publishes :3000 beyond this host.
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# --- Traces: Tempo ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Tempo NEEDS NO VARIABLE HERE - its config is observability/tempo/tempo.yaml, bind-mounted - and
# the two it involves are the SIDECAR'S, not this compose file's, so setting them in this file does
# nothing. They are listed because this is the stack they point at:
#
#   Observability__TraceOtlpEndpoint     where the sidecar pushes spans (OTLP/HTTP, the full /v1/traces
#                                        path, used as-is). appsettings.Development.json already sets
#                                        http://localhost:4318/v1/traces - this stack's loopback-published
#                                        receiver - so `dotnet run` needs nothing. Unset: no trace
#                                        exporter at all. Malformed: no exporter and one startup warning.
#                                        Unreachable: spans dropped, app unaffected.
#   Observability__TraceConsoleExporter  `true` also prints every span to stdout. Local debugging only -
#                                        off by default and set by no deployed environment, because
#                                        console spans reach whatever keeps the container's stdout.
#
# Ports, both on 127.0.0.1: 3200 is Tempo's query API (Grafana reads it as http://tempo:3200 over the
# compose network), 4318 its OTLP/HTTP receiver. Neither is a readiness dependency: /ready never
# probes Tempo.
# Observability__TraceOtlpEndpoint=http://localhost:4318/v1/traces
# Observability__TraceConsoleExporter=false
